WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:03.140
Hey David, let's talk about white privilege.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:10.700
Do poor white people have the same privilege
as rich white people?

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.490
I'm going to answer the question.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:19.130
It's not a hugely relevant one because the
question of privilege is better suited for

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:24.550
if you want to look at racial privilege, you
need to really look at it where as many other

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:29.830
variables as possible are held equal so that
you're really just looking at the variable

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:30.830
of race.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:36.399
So the question of whether a rich white person
has more or less privilege than a poor white

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:39.059
person is a fine question to ask.

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:40.219
The answer is easy.

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:44.290
A poor white person, white person has far
less privileged than a rich white person.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:47.159
I mean there there's a question that that's
your answer.

00:00:47.159 --> 00:00:51.410
It's not a very interesting question because
it doesn't really relate to the problems we're

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.319
trying to deal with in society.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:58.710
If everything other than skin color is the
same, then we can look at the specific effect

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.970
of skin color as a factor.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:06.840
And when you do that, if you imagine a poor
white person and a poor black person, if the

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:13.550
only difference is race, then there is some
degree of white privilege that the poor white

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.770
person has over the poor black person.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.250
And in fact there are some really interesting
studies with regard to employment where they

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.840
were for a job out jobs were open, low skilled
jobs, whatever.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.590
I think some were housekeeping, custodial
type work, other low skilled jobs, and really

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:35.860
big studies were done with thousands of resumes
with identical skills.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.980
Names were different, traditionally white
sounding names versus traditionally black

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.140
sounding names were sent out and the response
rates were tracked.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:50.520
And sometimes the white sounding name with
the same credentials as the black sounding

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:56.070
name would get as much as a 50% higher a callback
or interview rate.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.070
So that's an example.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.430
We're looking at low skill jobs.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:02.730
You're not talking about wealthy people or
even upper middle class or middle class people.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:11.330
Um, and you see that there is a specific difference
when varying only names that imply a certain

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:12.330
race.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.060
Okay, that's interesting to look at.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.230
Poor white versus rich white.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:21.069
Of course wealth confers privilege and wealth
confers advantage.

00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:26.730
Now I think it's also important to talk about
the reality that at a certain level of wealth,

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:33.510
wealth can start to override the lack of privilege
that might come from some other factor like

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.870
race or nationality or religion or gender
or whatever.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:38.870
Right?

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:43.680
There would be a certain point at which one
is so wealthy that regardless of their race,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:52.629
they are going to have a privilege in society
that will mostly make up for a characteristic

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:55.280
that would typically confer a disadvantage.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:02.700
Um, so the question is, is a fine one, if
you are sort of getting at the privilege that

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:09.489
wealth confers, but from the point of view
of thinking about OK, um, poor white people

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:14.680
and poor black people might live in different
neighborhoods, one of those neighborhoods

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:18.349
might be better connected with public transit.

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:24.920
That would be a form of privilege that would
relate to race, that would not necessarily

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:32.700
be, uh, obviously visible unless you really
try to fine tune and equate all but one variable,

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.599
isolate one variable and try to figure out
the differences there.

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:36.599
That's my thought.

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:42.279
A white privilege is different based on wealth,
but it's not a particularly interesting question.

00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:48.310
Since we know, particularly in this country,
wealth confers a ton of privilege.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:53.751
If I've misunderstood your question or you
disagree with my answer, by all means, let

00:03:53.751 --> 00:03:54.751
me know.

00:03:54.751 --> 00:03:56.520
We've got a great bonus show planned for you
today.

